:title("Python (subset) interpreter") ;
:description("Parses Python 3.12 - with significant indentation - and executes it
directly, without generating code. The goal is the whole practical language, and
tests/python-test-full.py is the ratchet that measures it: every section of that
file parses and passes, under goja and under -frozen alike.

Blocks are recognized with real INDENT/DEDENT handling: :script() rules keep an
indentation stack on the persistent parser stack, a suite is ':' NEWLINE INDENT
statements DEDENT, and every statement checks that it starts at the level of its
block. Comments (#) and blank lines are skipped by the newline rule. Indentation
must use spaces (no tabs).

The language: def (positional, keyword, default, *args, **kwargs, positional-only
and keyword-only parameters), lambda, nested defs and lexical closures (a function
captures the scope chain live at its definition), decorators including stacked ones,
factories and arbitrary expressions, classes with inheritance, dunder methods,
operator overloading, descriptors and the exception hierarchy, generators and
comprehensions of every kind (list, set, dict and generator expressions), the match
statement with its full pattern surface, try/except/else/finally, except*, raise,
with, assert, del, global and nonlocal, augmented and annotated assignment, chained
comparisons, conditional expressions, walrus, unpacking and starred targets, async
SYNTAX, type hint syntax (erased), and imports.

Values: int, float, complex (a boxed real/imag pair) and arbitrary precision
integers (a boxed sign and decimal limb array, entered only when a literal or a
result leaves the exact range of a double, and left again as soon as one fits),
bool, None, Ellipsis, str, bytes, list, tuple (modelled as a list), dict and set.
Numeric literals cover the 0x / 0o / 0b prefixes, underscore separators, leading
and trailing dot floats, exponents and the imaginary j suffix. String literals
cover every prefix (r, b, u and the rf/br orders), all four quote styles, adjacent
piece concatenation and the full 3.12 f-string surface: nesting, conversions,
format specs with nested fields, and the = debug form.

Scoping is Python's, not a chain search: a plain assignment inside a function or a
class body binds a LOCAL even when the module already has that name, and global /
nonlocal are the only way out (see pySet).

Known deviations, all deliberate:
  - Generators run by REPLAY. There are no coroutines in the value model, so every
    step re-runs the body from the top, feeds the yields that already happened the
    values they were sent, and stops at the next one. Same model as
    languages/js-interpreter.abnf; the two COMPILED halves have genuine coroutines
    (runtime.c's tag-15 cell) and match CPython exactly, so this is the one place
    the three engines are not the same machine.

    EXACTLY what differs, and it is only this: every side effect BEFORE the yield
    the generator is parked at happens once per resume instead of once, so the
    n-th step re-runs the first n-1 segments of the body and stepping a generator
    to exhaustion is O(n^2) in the body rather than O(n). A side effect on shared
    state therefore lands more than once - a list a generator appends to before
    its first yield ends up [1, 1, 2] where CPython leaves [1, 2].

    ORDER IS NOT AFFECTED. Measured, not assumed: an interleaving probe that
    numbers every driver event and every generator event into one log gives, after
    collapsing repeats, byte-identically CPython's sequence - for two generators
    driven alternately, for a generator mutating shared state, for try / with /
    finally nested around a yield, for close() mid-run, and for an exception
    object built before a yield and raised after it. That was NOT true before
    docs/todo.md 2.6 was worked: the suspension signal is a host-level throw, and
    it used to unwind the program's own try/finally and a with's __exit__ on its
    way out, so a generator's `finally` ran at the FIRST next() - before CPython
    runs it at all - and again at every step after that. makeTry, runWith and
    genClose now let that signal pass through untouched (isGenSuspend), and
    close() replays to the parked yield and raises there, which is how the
    `finally` runs at the moment CPython runs it.

    STILL MISSING, and it is not reachable from here: gen.throw(). The record
    would travel on the resume value exactly as close()'s exit record does, but
    the COMPILED halves cannot follow - a generator there is the C floor's tag-15
    cell whose member table is host-dispatched and offers next and return only, so
    giving python a .throw() the two halves agree on is a runtime.c change.
    languages/js-interpreter.abnf records the same wall for the same reason. For
    the same reason a close() is not offered to `except`: the floor's GEN_EXIT
    sentinel unwinds `finally` clauses without being catchable, so neither engine
    lets a generator catch its own GeneratorExit, where CPython does.
  - A tuple is a list, so it is mutable and compares equal to a list.
  - A complex whose imaginary part is zero is an ordinary number, and int / float
    are one numeric type, so type() cannot tell 1 from 1.0 or from 1+0j.
  - An imported module binds its names flat, without a module object, so the
    from-import style is the one that works.
  - async functions are defined and inspected, never awaited: there is no event
    loop.
  - The BUILT-IN method surface is a subset, and hasattr() answers exactly what
    the dispatcher can run: list has all eleven of its methods, str has the
    library in abnf/pystrmethod.go, dict has keys/values/items/get/pop/clear/
    copy/setdefault/update, and set has add/pop/clear/copy/remove/discard/union/
    update - so hasattr of an int and bit_length is False here and True in CPython, and
    a set's intersection/difference/issubset are still only the OPERATORS & - <=.
    A bound method from getattr() does NOT carry keyword arguments:
    getattr(xs, sort)(reverse=True) is not xs.sort(reverse=True), and
    getattr(d, update)(a=1) is not d.update(a=1); the written calls are.

There is no main() requirement: the file runs top to bottom, and a main() is called
afterwards when one is defined. Self checking programs end with exit(fails[0]) or
return the failure count from main().") ;


// --- main rules

// Shared token productions (languages/lib).
// Identifiers, INLINED from lib/ident.abnf and widened: a Python identifier may hold
// non-ASCII letters (PEP 3131), and CPython's test_fstring writes `ten<pi> = 31.4`
// deliberately, "to trigger the code in ast.c that deals with non-ascii expression
// values". The shared lib rule is ASCII-only and every other language here wants it that
// way, so the widened copy lives with the language that needs it. The range starts at
// \x7f, not \x80: c.peek reads BYTES and \x80 is a UTF-8 continuation byte, which
// matches nothing (docs/abnf-dialect-gotchas.md). This is a RECOGNITION rule, not
// XID_Start - it accepts any non-ASCII byte in a name, which is wider than Python.
// KwEnd is widened the same way, so a keyword glued to a non-ASCII letter (`in<pi>`)
// stays one identifier rather than a keyword plus a name.
Id          = ( "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" | "_" | "\x7f"..."\U0010ffff" ) :whitespace() { "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" | "0"..."9" | "_" | "\x7f"..."\U0010ffff" } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
KwEnd = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var cc = c.peek(0)
        return ((cc >= 48 && cc <= 57) || (cc >= 65 && cc <= 90) || cc === 95
                || (cc >= 97 && cc <= 122) || cc >= 127)
            ? abnf.newToken("\x01keyword-boundary", 0)
            : undefined
    })()
~~) ;
:include("lib/dq-strings.abnf") ;
:include("lib/numbers.abnf") ;

:startRule(Program) ;
:whitespace(Whitespace) ;

// The leading NL swallows any comment/blank lines at the top of the file.
Program     <~~ runProgram(takeAll()) ~~>
            = [ NL ] { TopStmt } ;
// Imports are module level (column 0) and yield no statement, so they are tried
// at the TopStmt level (before Statement) and push nothing net.
TopStmt     = SameLevel ( ImportLine | Statement ) ;
ImportLine  = ( Import | FromImport ) NL ;

// Module level imports. resolveImport (lib/interp-core.js) checks each module
// path against core.stdlibImports; an unresolvable one aborts (or, under
// -warn-imports, warns and continues). An import produces no statement thunk:
// DottedName pushes the bare path and the resolving tag pops it (net zero).
Import      = KwImport ImportMod { "," ImportMod } ;
ImportMod   <~~ resolveImport(pop(), up.pos) ~~>
            = DottedName [ KwAs Id ] ;
FromImport  <~~ resolveImport(pop(), up.pos) ~~>
            = KwFrom RelName KwImport ( "*" | ImportParen | ImportName { "," ImportName } ) ;
// from . import x / from ..pkg import y: the leading dots are the relative package
// level, which this flat import model has nothing to resolve against, so they are
// accepted and the name that follows (if any) decides.
RelName     <~~ push(stripWs(up.in)) ~~>
            = DotRun NotImportKw Id { "." Id } | DotRun ;
DotRun      = { "." } ;
// `from . import x` is the ordered-choice trap of the top of docs/abnf-dialect-gotchas.md:
// the first alternative's Id happily matches the word `import` itself, RelName COMMITS to
// the reading `from (.import)`, and the parse then dies on the name after it - PEG never
// re-enters RelName to try the bare-dots alternative. A `!"import"` lookahead is wrong
// here because it would also reject `from .importlib import x`, so the guard checks the
// word boundary too and only fails on `import` as a whole word.
NotImportKw = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var i = 0
        while (i < 4096) {
            var ws = c.peek(i)
            if (ws === 32 || ws === 9) { i++; continue }
            break
        }
        var kw = "import"
        for (var k = 0; k < 6; k++) {
            if (c.peek(i + k) !== kw.charCodeAt(k)) { return undefined }
        }
        var after = c.peek(i + 6)
        if ((after >= 48 && after <= 57) || (after >= 65 && after <= 90) ||
            after === 95 || (after >= 97 && after <= 122)) { return undefined }
        return abnf.newToken("\x01import-kw", 0)
    })()
~~) ;
// The parenthesized (and therefore multi-line) form of the name list.
ImportParen = "(" :whitespace(WhitespaceNL) ImportName { "," ImportName } [ "," ] ")" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
ImportName  = Id [ KwAs Id ] ;
DottedName  <~~ push(stripWs(up.in)) ~~>
            = Id { "." Id } ;
KwImport    = "import" KwEnd ;
KwFrom      = "from" KwEnd ;
KwAs        = "as" KwEnd ;

// Compound statements own their suites; small statements end with a newline.
Statement   = If | While | For | TryStmt | With | Match | Class | Decorated | AsyncDef | AsyncFor | AsyncWith | Def | SmallLine ;

SmallLine   = SmallStmt { ";" SmallStmt } [ ";" ] NL ;
// An import may sit inside a function, a try or an if - CPython's own test suite
// is full of them - so it is a SMALL statement too, not only a module level one.
SmallStmt   = Import | FromImport | TypeAliasStmt | Return | Break | Continue | Pass | Raise | Assert | Global | Nonlocal | Del | YieldStmt
            | AugAssign | AnnAssign | Assign | ExprSmall ;

// A suite: ':' NEWLINE INDENT statements DEDENT. Every further statement of the
// suite starts at the same level; the first one defined the level via Indent.
// A suite is either an indented block - ':' NEWLINE INDENT statements DEDENT, every
// further statement at the same level - or the ONE-LINE form, ':' followed by small
// statements on the same line (def f(self): return 1, if c: t = 5).
Suite       <~~ push(makeSeq(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ":" ( NL Indent Statement { SameLevel Statement } Dedent | SmallLine ) ;

// match SUBJECT: case PATTERN [if GUARD]: SUITE ... - the structural pattern match of
// PEP 634. `match` and `case` are soft keywords, so a Match only commits once the
// `:` NEWLINE INDENT `case` shape is there.
Match       <~~ push(makeMatch(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "match" KwEnd ExprList ":" NL Indent CaseClause { SameLevel CaseClause } Dedent ;
CaseClause  <~~ push(makeCase(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "case" KwEnd Patterns [ CaseGuard ] Suite ;
CaseGuard   <~~ push({guard: pop()}) ~~>
            = "if" KwEnd Expression ;
// A comma-separated pattern list is a sequence pattern (case 1, 2:); a single
// pattern with no comma stays itself.
Patterns    <~~ push(makeSeqPattern(takeAll(), false)) ~~>
            = OrPattern { "," <~~ push({pcomma: true}) ~~> OrPattern } [ "," <~~ push({pcomma: true}) ~~> ] ;
OrPattern   <~~ push(makeOrPattern(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ClosedPattern { "|" ClosedPattern } [ AsPattern ] ;
AsPattern   = KwAs Id <~~ push({asname: up.in}) ~~> ;
ClosedPattern = GroupPattern | SeqPatternB | MapPattern | ClassPattern | ValuePattern | LitPattern | StarPattern | WildPattern | CapturePattern ;
GroupPattern <~~ push(makeSeqPattern(takeAll(), false)) ~~>
            = "(" :whitespace(WhitespaceNL) [ OrPattern { "," <~~ push({pcomma: true}) ~~> [ OrPattern ] } ] ")" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
SeqPatternB <~~ push(makeSeqPattern(takeAll(), true)) ~~>
            = "[" :whitespace(WhitespaceNL) [ OrPattern { "," OrPattern } [ "," ] ] "]" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
MapPattern  <~~ push(makeMapPattern(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "{" :whitespace(WhitespaceNL) [ MapItem { "," MapItem } [ "," ] ] "}" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
MapItem     = MapRest | MapEntry ;
MapRest     <~~ push({maprest: up.in}) ~~>
            = "**" Id ;
MapEntry    <~~ var p2 = pop(); push({mkey: pop(), mpat: p2}) ~~>
            = Expression ":" OrPattern ;
ClassPattern <~~ push(makeClassPattern(takeAll())) ~~>
            = DottedRef "(" :whitespace(WhitespaceNL) [ ClassPatArg { "," ClassPatArg } [ "," ] ] ")" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
ClassPatArg = ClassPatKw | ClassPatPos ;
ClassPatKw  <~~ var p3 = pop(); push({ckey: pop(), cpat: p3}) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> "=" NotEqSign OrPattern ;
ClassPatPos <~~ push({cpos: pop()}) ~~>
            = OrPattern ;
// A dotted name in a pattern is a VALUE pattern (case K.LIMIT:), a bare name a
// capture; both need the class-pattern form to be tried first.
ValuePattern <~~ push(makeValuePattern(pop())) ~~>
            = DottedRef2 ;
DottedRef   <~~ push(makeVarRef(stripWs(up.in))) ~~>
            = Id { "." Id } ;
DottedRef2  <~~ push(makeDotted(stripWs(up.in))) ~~>
            = Id "." Id { "." Id } ;
LitPattern  <~~ push(makeValuePattern(pop())) ~~>
            = ( StringLit | NegNumLit | PyNumLit | True | False | None ) ;
NegNumLit   <~~ push(makeNeg(pop())) ~~>
            = "-" PyNumLit ;
StarPattern <~~ push({pstar: up.in}) ~~>
            = "*" Id ;
WildPattern <~~ push({wild: true}) ~~>
            = "_" KwEnd ;
CapturePattern <~~ push({capture: up.in}) ~~>
            = Id ;

If          <~~ push(makeIf(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "if" KwEnd Expression Suite { Elif } [ Else ] ;
Elif        <~~ push(makeElif(pop(), pop())) ~~>
            = SameLevel "elif" KwEnd Expression Suite ;
Else        <~~ push(makeElse(pop())) ~~>
            = SameLevel "else" KwEnd Suite ;

// A loop `else` suite runs when the loop finished without a break.
While       <~~ push(makeWhileElse(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "while" KwEnd NamedTest Suite [ LoopElse ] ;
LoopElse    <~~ push({loopelse: pop()}) ~~>
            = SameLevel "else" KwEnd Suite ;

// for x in ITER: a single name (genuinely executed). A tuple target
// (for a, b in ...  /  for (a, b) in ...) is accepted and reported as not
// implemented (the subset has no tuple unpacking in the loop head).
// for TARGETS in ITER: the target is a full target list, so `for head, *tail in xs`
// unpacks each element. An `else` suite runs when the loop was not broken.
For         <~~ push(makeForStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "for" KwEnd TargetList "in" KwEnd ExprList Suite [ LoopElse ] ;

// genPush/genPop bracket the body so makeYield can mark the def as a GENERATOR
// function (calling it then yields a generator object rather than running the body).
Def         <~~ push(makeDef(pop(), pop(), pop(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = "def" KwEnd Id <~~ push(up.in); genPush() ~~> [ SkipBracket ] ParamList [ "->" DiscardType ] Suite ;
// Parameter lists: plain names, default values (evaluated once, when the def
// statement runs - like real Python) and a *args rest parameter all work;
// **kwargs, the bare '*' and '/' markers and keyword-only parameters after
// *args are reported as not implemented. Annotations parse and are ignored.
ParamList   <~~ push(makeParamList(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "(" :whitespace(WhitespaceNL) [ Param { "," Param } [ "," ] ] ")" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
// The full Python parameter grammar: positional-or-keyword names with defaults, the
// `/` marker that ends the positional-ONLY group, `*args` (or a bare `*`) after which
// every name is keyword-ONLY, and a final `**kwargs`.
Param       = DblStarParam | StarParam | SlashParam | NamedParam ;
DblStarParam <~~ push({pkwrest: up.in}) ~~>
            = "**" Id [ ":" DiscardType ] ;
StarParam   <~~ push(makeRestParam(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "*" [ Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ ":" DiscardType ] ] ;
SlashParam  <~~ push({pslash: true}) ~~>
            = "/" ;
NamedParam  <~~ push(makeNamedParam(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ ":" DiscardType ] [ ParamDefault ] ;
ParamDefault <~~ push({pdefault: pop()}) ~~>
            = "=" Expression ;

// A decorated def / async-def / class: the decorators parse (their call
// expressions are dropped) and the function/class is defined unwrapped.
// Decorators are applied bottom-up to the defined name, exactly as in Python; PEP 614
// allows any expression, so the decorator is a full expression (registry["t"]).
Decorated   <~~ push(makeDecorated(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Decorator { SameLevel Decorator } SameLevel ( Def | AsyncDef | Class ) ;
Decorator   <~~ push({deco: pop()}) ~~>
            = "@" Expression NL ;

// class Name[(bases, metaclass=..., ...)]: SUITE - accepted structurally and
// reported as not implemented (the subset has no user-defined classes). The base
// list reuses the call-argument surface (positional bases, keyword and *unpacked
// arguments); the whole body is parsed and dropped.
// class Name[[T, ...]][(bases, ...)]: SUITE. The body is a real suite run in a fresh
// namespace scope, so plain statements (loops, conditionals) inside a class body work
// and every name it binds - including its defs - becomes a class attribute.
Class       <~~ push(makeClass(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "class" KwEnd Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ SkipBracket ] [ ClassBases ] Suite ;
ClassBases  <~~ push({bases: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "(" :whitespace(WhitespaceNL) [ CallArg { "," CallArg } [ "," ] ] ")" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
// A PEP 695 type-parameter list, [T], [A, B] - parsed and dropped (types are erased).
SkipBracket = "[" { SkipBracket | SkipBracketItem } "]" ;
SkipBracketItem = "\x00"..."\x5a" | "\x5c" | "\x5e"..."\U0010ffff" ;

// async def / await: accepted structurally, reported as not implemented.
// async def / async for / async with / await: the SYNTAX is genuine - an async
// function is an ordinary function value here, so it can be defined, named, decorated
// and inspected. There is no event loop, so awaiting merely evaluates its operand;
// running real coroutines is out of the subset.
AsyncDef    <~~ push(pop()) ~~>
            = "async" KwEnd Def ;
AsyncFor    <~~ push(pop()) ~~>
            = "async" KwEnd For ;
AsyncWith   <~~ push(pop()) ~~>
            = "async" KwEnd With ;

// try/except/else/finally: accepted; under -warn-unsupported the try body (and a
// finally body) run, so calls inside them stay visible. There is no exception
// model, so the except/else handlers are parsed and dropped.
TryStmt     <~~ push(makeTry(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "try" KwEnd TryBody ( ExceptClause { ExceptClause } [ TryElse ] [ TryFinally ] | TryFinally ) ;
TryBody     <~~ push({trybody: pop()}) ~~>
            = Suite ;
// except [TYPE [as NAME]]: / except* TYPE as NAME: - the handler type is a real
// expression (a class, or a tuple of classes) and decides which clause runs.
ExceptClause <~~ push(makeExcept(takeAll())) ~~>
            = SameLevel "except" KwEnd [ "*" <~~ push({star: true}) ~~> ] [ ExceptType [ KwAs Id <~~ push({hname: up.in}) ~~> ] ] Suite ;
// PEP 758 (Python 3.14): the exception list may be written WITHOUT parentheses when
// there is no `as` clause - `except EOFError, TypeError, ZeroDivisionError:`. Taking an
// expression LIST here accepts both that and the parenthesized form; the tuple it builds
// is the same one the parenthesized spelling produces, so makeExcept is unchanged.
ExceptType  <~~ push({htype: pop()}) ~~>
            = ExprList ;
TryElse     <~~ push({elsebody: pop()}) ~~>
            = SameLevel "else" KwEnd Suite ;
TryFinally  <~~ push({finalbody: pop()}) ~~>
            = SameLevel "finally" KwEnd Suite ;

// raise: accepted, reported as not implemented; the raised expression still runs.
// raise EXPR [from CAUSE] and the bare `raise` that re-raises the active exception.
Raise       <~~ push(makeRaise(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "raise" KwEnd [ Expression [ KwFrom RaiseCause ] ] ;
RaiseCause  <~~ push({cause: pop()}) ~~>
            = Expression ;

// with EXPR [as TARGET]: a simple form - the context expression runs, the target
// is bound to its value and the body runs (no __enter__/__exit__ protocol), so it
// is reported as not implemented but stays visible under -warn-unsupported.
// with EXPR [as TARGET], ...: the real context-manager protocol - __enter__ before
// the body, __exit__ after it (also on the way out of an exception, and a truthy
// result swallows it). The 3.10 parenthesized item list is accepted too.
With        <~~ push(makeWith(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "with" KwEnd ( WithParen | WithItem { "," WithItem } ) WithBody ;
WithParen   = "(" :whitespace(WhitespaceNL) WithItem { "," WithItem } [ "," ] ")" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
WithItem    <~~ push(makeWithItem(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Expression [ KwAs TargetItem ] ;
WithBody    <~~ push({withbody: pop()}) ~~>
            = Suite ;

// assert COND [, MSG]: genuinely implemented - a false condition aborts the run.
Assert      <~~ push(makeAssert(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "assert" KwEnd Expression [ "," Expression ] ;

// global / nonlocal: the declaration guarantees a binding in the right scope,
// then plain assignment (update where the chain resolves the name) lands there.
// Deviation: a nearer binding of the same name still wins over the declared
// scope (real Python forces it); nonlocal is checked when the call reaches it,
// not at compile time. del stays unsupported.
Global      <~~ push(makeGlobal(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = "global" KwEnd Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> } ;
Nonlocal    <~~ push(makeNonlocal(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = "nonlocal" KwEnd Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> } ;
// del NAME / del obj.attr / del seq[i] / del seq[a:b:c]: the binding or the element
// is genuinely removed, so a later read of the name raises like Python's
// UnboundLocalError.
// `del y,` (trailing comma), `del (z)` and even `del ()` are all legal, and test_grammar
// checks each of them.
Del         <~~ push(makeDel(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "del" KwEnd DelTarget { "," DelTarget } [ "," ] ;
DelTarget   = "(" :whitespace(WhitespaceNL) [ Target { "," Target } [ "," ] ] ")" :whitespace(Whitespace) | Target ;

// yield: accepted as a statement, reported as not implemented (no generators).
YieldStmt   <~~ push(exprToStmt(pop())) ~~>
            = YieldExpr ;



// name: Type [= value]: the annotation is parsed and ignored; with a value it is a
// plain assignment, without one it is a no-op declaration.
// name: Type [= value]. The annotation is erased, but the NAME is recorded so a class
// body can answer __annotations__; with a value it is a plain assignment.
// name: Type [= value]. The annotation is erased, but the target's NAME is recorded
// so a class body can answer __annotations__; with a value it is a plain assignment.
AnnAssign   <~~ push(makeAnnAssign(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Target ":" AnnType [ "=" ExprList ] ;
AnnType     <~~ push({atype: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = DiscardType ;

// An annotation is an arbitrary EXPRESSION in Python, not a little type language of its
// own: `def f(x: not (int is int), /)`, `no_name[does_not_exist]: 1/0 = 0` and
// `type Alias[T] = lambda: T` are all real annotations, and CPython's own suite tests all
// three. A hand-written type grammar can never catch up with that, so the annotation is
// parsed as an ordinary expression and DROPPED - annotations here are inspected as TEXT
// (stripWs(up.in)) and never evaluated. The tag pops the one thunk Expression pushes, so
// the production stays value-stack-neutral, which is what a parameter list requires.
DiscardType <~~ pop() ~~>
            = Expression ;

// type Name[T] = TYPE (PEP 695): the alias is a value carrying __name__ and
// __value__, so it can be inspected; types themselves are erased.
TypeAliasStmt <~~ push(makeTypeAlias(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "type" KwEnd Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ SkipBracket ] "=" TypeValue ;
TypeValue   <~~ push(makeTypeValue(stripWs(up.in))) ~~>
            = DiscardType ;

// `return a, b` returns a TUPLE, so the value is a full expression LIST.
Return      <~~ push(makeReturn(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "return" KwEnd [ ExprList ] ;
Break       <~~ push(makeBreak()) ~~>    = "break" KwEnd ;
Continue    <~~ push(makeContinue()) ~~> = "continue" KwEnd ;
Pass        <~~ push(function() {}) ~~>  = "pass" KwEnd ;

// The augmented assignments, all of Python's. `//=` and `**=` must be tried before
// `/=` and `*=`.
AugAssign   <~~ push(makeAugAssign(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Target ( "+=" | "-=" | "**=" | "*=" | "//=" | "/=" | "%=" | "@="
                     | "|=" | "&=" | "^=" | "<<=" | ">>=" ) <~~ push({op: up.in}) ~~> ExprList ;
